From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 14:09:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Address object stores and indexes by id, from a
 metadata snapshot

Script knew only the names of a connection's object stores, from the
open result, and each IDBObjectStore handle asked the backend for its
key path, key generator and indexes with a synchronous GetObjectStore.
Every request, transaction scope and schema operation named its store,
and renaming a store only changed script's state: the backend never
learnt about it, so the renamed store lost its records and indexes.

Now the open and upgrade results (ConnectionMsg::Connection and
Upgrade) carry the full metadata snapshot, the committed metadata the
engine keeps since P1: object stores with id, name, key path and key
generator, and their indexes with id, name, key path, unique and
multiEntry (design decision 8). IDBDatabase keeps it as the
connection's object store set and builds IDBObjectStore and IDBIndex
handles from it, so objectStore() no longer asks the backend;
GetObjectStore and the unused synchronous Version message are gone,
and script's IndexedDB code has no synchronous IPC left. Requests,
transaction scopes and schema operations address stores, and schema
operations indexes, by id, end to end to the queries.

In an upgrade transaction, script gives a new store or index one more
than the largest id the connection has seen in the transaction,
seeded from the snapshot and never decreasing, so a store deleted and
created again under its name in one upgrade is a new store while
handles of both exist, and sends the schema operations without
waiting. The backend checks them: a new id must be larger than every
id in its table, the store and index of other operations must exist,
and names must be free; otherwise the operation fails its
transaction, as any failed write does, and its callback makes script
abort it. Renaming a store or an index is a schema operation that
updates its row (RenameObjectStore, RenameIndex by id), so records and
indexes stay with it. Deleting an index also deletes its rows in
index_data and unique_index_data. The schema and the record format
are unchanged, the ids were the tables' primary keys already, so
SCHEMA_VERSION stays 1.

Upgrade transactions change the connection's metadata in script, and
"abort an upgrade transaction" restores the snapshot taken when the
transaction was created. Handles revert from it by id: a store's name
unless the transaction created the store, its index set, reusing the
index handles by id, and now also index handle names (step 6).
deleteObjectStore empties the store handle's index set (step 6),
handles are found by store id rather than by name, and a handle whose
store or index was deleted stays deleted when another one takes its
name. IDBTransaction.objectStoreNames of an upgrade transaction is
sorted now, as the spec asks.

Tests: the metadata snapshot with ids, renames by id (a swap, records
and key generator kept), and the refused operations; a schema
operation with an id that isn't new, or for a missing store or index,
or a taken name, fails its transaction, which rolls back; the
aborted-upgrade test also renames a store; the integration tests use
ids and read the metadata from a new connection's snapshot, and a
duplicate store name now fails the upgrade. A fork WPT test,
tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/upgrade-metadata-by-id.any.js,
covers a store and an index deleted and created again in one upgrade,
swapped store names, and store and index renames reverted by an
abort. The integration tests' open helper ignores TxnMaybeCommit, which
can reach a test's connection before its Commit, as a DAYU200 run
showed.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 107 passed, 1 skipped (P1's tip: 105). DAYU200 `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage`: RDB only 108 passed, 3 skipped; SQLite and RDB 186 passed, 4 skipped. `./mach check --ohos` of servo-storage with SQLite, RDB and both, `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings` and `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --use-crown --locked --profile checked-release`: no warning (crown's unrooted_must_root needs the object store names of IDBDatabase::object_store_names in a rooted_vec); `./mach test-tidy`: only the known libsqlite3-sys advisory. WPT IndexedDB/ (4,511 subtests): host SpiderMonkey 3,278 pass (P1's tip: 3,251), host QuickJS 3,281 (3,253), DAYU200 with QuickJS and RDB 3,272 (3,247); the new passes, idbobjectstore-rename-store and idbtransaction_objectStoreNames during an upgrade, 10 of 10 with `--repeat 10` on both host engines; the other unexpected results are known flakes, and the board's new ones passed 5 of 5 alone. /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ with the new test: 62 of 62 on both host engines.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote the change and the tests, and ran the host, device and WPT runs; after the P2 review it documented that store and index ids can be reused)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
